Let $f$ be a function defined on a domain $D$.

\textbf{Absolute Maximum:} $f(c)$ is the \textbf{absolute maximum} of $f$ on $D$ if:
\[
\boxed{f(c) \geq f(x) \quad \text{for all } x \in D}
\]

\textbf{Absolute Minimum:} $f(c)$ is the \textbf{absolute minimum} of $f$ on $D$ if:
\[
\boxed{f(c) \leq f(x) \quad \text{for all } x \in D}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Key Terminology:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Absolute maximum and minimum together are called \textbf{absolute extrema} or \textbf{global extrema}
    \item The extremum is a \textbf{value} (the $y$-coordinate), not the point itself
    \item A function may have only one absolute maximum and one absolute minimum
    \item Some functions may not attain absolute extrema if the domain is not closed or bounded
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Important:}}
The absolute maximum \textit{value} is the largest $y$-value, not the $x$-value where it occurs!

\vspace{0.5cm}

If $f$ is \textbf{continuous} on a \textbf{closed interval} $[a, b]$, then $f$ attains both an absolute maximum and an absolute minimum on $[a, b]$.

That is, there exist numbers $c$ and $d$ in $[a, b]$ such that:
\[
\boxed{f(c) \leq f(x) \leq f(d) \quad \text{for all } x \in [a, b]}
\]

where $f(c)$ is the absolute minimum and $f(d)$ is the absolute maximum.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Two Essential Conditions:}}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $f$ must be \textbf{continuous} on $[a, b]$
    \item The interval must be \textbf{closed} (includes both endpoints $a$ and $b$)
\end{enumerate}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{If Either Condition Fails, EVT May Not Hold:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $f(x) = \dfrac{1}{x}$ on $(0, 1)$: open interval $\to$ no minimum exists
    \item $f(x) = \tan x$ on $[0, \frac{\pi}{2}]$: discontinuous at $\frac{\pi}{2}$ $\to$ no maximum exists
    \item $f(x) = x$ on $(-\infty, \infty)$: unbounded domain $\to$ no absolute extrema
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

A number $c$ in the domain of $f$ is called a \textbf{critical number} (or critical point) of $f$ if either:
\[
\boxed{f'(c) = 0} \quad \textbf{OR} \quad \boxed{f'(c) \text{ does not exist}}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Fermat's Theorem:}}

If $f$ has a local maximum or minimum at $c$, and $f'(c)$ exists, then $f'(c) = 0$.

\textbf{Contrapositive:} If $f'(c) \neq 0$, then $f$ does not have a local extremum at $c$.

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Converse is FALSE:}}

Not every critical number gives a local extremum! For example, $f(x) = x^3$ has $f'(0) = 0$, but $x = 0$ is neither a max nor a min.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Types of Critical Numbers:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $f'(c) = 0$: horizontal tangent (stationary point)
    \item $f'(c)$ undefined: corner, cusp, or vertical tangent
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

To find the \textbf{absolute maximum and minimum} of a continuous function $f$ on a closed interval $[a, b]$:

\textbf{Step 1:} Find all critical numbers of $f$ in the \textit{open} interval $(a, b)$
\begin{itemize}
    \item Solve $f'(x) = 0$
    \item Find where $f'(x)$ does not exist
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 2:} Evaluate $f$ at each critical number found in Step 1

\textbf{Step 3:} Evaluate $f$ at the endpoints: $f(a)$ and $f(b)$

\textbf{Step 4:} Compare all values from Steps 2 and 3:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The \textbf{largest} value is the absolute maximum
    \item The \textbf{smallest} value is the absolute minimum
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Critical Warning:}}

\textbf{NEVER forget the endpoints!} Absolute extrema can occur at endpoints even when there are critical numbers inside the interval.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find all critical numbers of:

(a) $f(x) = 2x^3 - 3x^2 - 12x + 5$

(b) $g(x) = x^{2/3}$

(c) $h(x) = |x - 2|$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} $f(x) = 2x^3 - 3x^2 - 12x + 5$

Find $f'(x)$:
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= 6x^2 - 6x - 12 \\
&= 6(x^2 - x - 2) \\
&= 6(x - 2)(x + 1)
\end{align*}

Set $f'(x) = 0$:
\[
6(x-2)(x+1) = 0 \implies x = 2 \text{ or } x = -1
\]

$f'(x)$ exists for all $x$.

\textbf{Critical numbers:} \boxed{x = 2, \quad x = -1}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} $g(x) = x^{2/3}$

Find $g'(x)$:
\begin{align*}
g'(x) &= \frac{2}{3}x^{-1/3} = \frac{2}{3x^{1/3}}
\end{align*}

$g'(x) = 0$: Never (numerator is always 2)

$g'(x)$ undefined: When $3x^{1/3} = 0$, i.e., when $x = 0$

\textbf{Critical number:} \boxed{x = 0} (where derivative doesn't exist)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(c)} $h(x) = |x - 2|$

Rewrite: $h(x) = \begin{cases} -(x-2) & x < 2 \\ x-2 & x \geq 2 \end{cases}$

Therefore: $h'(x) = \begin{cases} -1 & x < 2 \\ 1 & x > 2 \\ \text{undefined} & x = 2 \end{cases}$

$h'(x) = 0$: Never

$h'(x)$ undefined: At $x = 2$

\textbf{Critical number:} \boxed{x = 2} (corner point)

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of $f(x) = x^2 - 1$ on $[-1, 2]$.

(From 2021 past paper, Q4 part b)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find critical numbers in $(-1, 2)$

\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= 2x \\
f'(x) = 0 &\implies x = 0
\end{align*}

Check: $0 \in (-1, 2)$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Step 2:} Evaluate at critical number

\[
f(0) = 0^2 - 1 = -1
\]

\textbf{Step 3:} Evaluate at endpoints

\begin{align*}
f(-1) &= (-1)^2 - 1 = 1 - 1 = 0 \\
f(2) &= 2^2 - 1 = 4 - 1 = 3
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Compare all values

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{$x$} & \textbf{$f(x)$} & \textbf{Type} \\
\hline
$-1$ & $0$ & Endpoint \\
\hline
$0$ & $-1$ & Critical Number \\
\hline
$2$ & $3$ & Endpoint \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Answer:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Absolute Maximum:} \boxed{f(2) = 3 \text{ at } x = 2}
    \item \textbf{Absolute Minimum:} \boxed{f(0) = -1 \text{ at } x = 0}
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Geometric Check:}} This is a parabola $y = x^2 - 1$ opening upward with vertex at $(0, -1)$. On $[-1, 2]$, the minimum occurs at the vertex and the maximum at the rightmost endpoint. \checkmark

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of $f(x) = 2x^3 - 3x^2 - 12x + 1$ on $[-2, 3]$.

(From 2025 past paper, Q4)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find critical numbers

\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= 6x^2 - 6x - 12 \\
&= 6(x^2 - x - 2) \\
&= 6(x - 2)(x + 1)
\end{align*}

Setting $f'(x) = 0$:
\[
x = 2 \quad \text{or} \quad x = -1
\]

Both $x = 2$ and $x = -1$ lie in $(-2, 3)$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Step 2:} Evaluate at critical numbers

\begin{align*}
f(2) &= 2(8) - 3(4) - 12(2) + 1 \\
&= 16 - 12 - 24 + 1 \\
&= -19 \\
\\
f(-1) &= 2(-1) - 3(1) - 12(-1) + 1 \\
&= -2 - 3 + 12 + 1 \\
&= 8
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Evaluate at endpoints

\begin{align*}
f(-2) &= 2(-8) - 3(4) - 12(-2) + 1 \\
&= -16 - 12 + 24 + 1 \\
&= -3 \\
\\
f(3) &= 2(27) - 3(9) - 12(3) + 1 \\
&= 54 - 27 - 36 + 1 \\
&= -8
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Compare all values

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{$x$} & \textbf{$f(x)$} & \textbf{Type} \\
\hline
$-2$ & $-3$ & Endpoint \\
\hline
$-1$ & $8$ & Critical Number \\
\hline
$2$ & $-19$ & Critical Number \\
\hline
$3$ & $-8$ & Endpoint \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Answer:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Absolute Maximum:} \boxed{f(-1) = 8 \text{ at } x = -1}
    \item \textbf{Absolute Minimum:} \boxed{f(2) = -19 \text{ at } x = 2}
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Verification:}} Maximum value: 8 is largest among $\{-3, 8, -19, -8\}$ \checkmark

Minimum value: $-19$ is smallest \checkmark

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 4.1 Summary}}

\textbf{Extreme Value Theorem:}
\[
f \text{ continuous on } [a,b] \implies f \text{ attains absolute max and min on } [a,b]
\]

\textbf{Closed Interval Method -- Finding Absolute Extrema:}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Find all critical numbers in $(a,b)$: solve $f'(x) = 0$ and find where $f'$ is undefined
    \item Evaluate $f$ at each critical number
    \item Evaluate $f$ at both endpoints $a$ and $b$
    \item Largest value = Absolute Maximum
    \item Smallest value = Absolute Minimum
\end{enumerate}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Most Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{red}{\textbf{Forgetting to check endpoints}} -- absolute extrema often occur there!
    \item Confusing local extrema with absolute extrema
    \item Not verifying that critical numbers lie inside $(a,b)$
    \item Stating the $x$-coordinate instead of the $y$-value as the extremum
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Quick Memory Aid:}} EVT = Closed + Continuous $\implies$ Extrema Exist

\newpage

If a function $f$ satisfies \textbf{all three} conditions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $f$ is \textbf{continuous} on the closed interval $[a, b]$
    \item $f$ is \textbf{differentiable} on the open interval $(a, b)$
    \item $f(a) = f(b)$ (equal function values at endpoints)
\end{enumerate}

Then there exists at least one number $c$ in the open interval $(a, b)$ such that:
\[
\boxed{f'(c) = 0}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Geometric Interpretation:}}

If a smooth curve starts and ends at the same height, there must be at least one point in between where the tangent line is horizontal (slope = 0).

\vspace{0.5cm}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.3]
    % Axes
    \draw[->] (-0.5, 0) -- (6, 0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->] (0, -0.5) -- (0, 3.5) node[above] {$y$};

    % Curve satisfying Rolle's Theorem
    \draw[primaryblue, thick, domain=0.5:5.5, samples=100] plot (\x, {-0.3*(\x-1.5)*(\x-3)*(\x-4.5) + 1.5});

    % Endpoints at same height
    \filldraw[red] (0.5, 1.5) circle (2pt) node[below left] {$(a, f(a))$};
    \filldraw[red] (5.5, 1.5) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$(b, f(b))$};

    % Points where derivative is zero
    \filldraw[darkgreen] (2.2, 2.7) circle (2pt) node[above] {$c_1$};
    \filldraw[darkgreen] (4.3, 0.6) circle (2pt) node[below] {$c_2$};

    % Horizontal tangent lines
    \draw[accentorange, dashed] (1.5, 2.7) -- (2.9, 2.7) node[right] {$f'(c_1) = 0$};
    \draw[accentorange, dashed] (3.6, 0.6) -- (5, 0.6) node[right] {$f'(c_2) = 0$};

    % Horizontal line showing equal heights
    \draw[gray, dotted, thick] (0.5, 1.5) -- (5.5, 1.5);

    % Axis labels
    \node at (0.5, -0.3) {$a$};
    \node at (5.5, -0.3) {$b$};
    \node at (2.2, -0.3) {$c_1$};
    \node at (4.3, -0.3) {$c_2$};

    % Title
    \node[align=center, primaryblue] at (3, -1.2) {\textbf{Rolle's Theorem: Multiple horizontal tangents possible}\\$f(a) = f(b) \implies \exists\, c \in (a,b): f'(c) = 0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.5cm}

All three conditions are \textbf{essential}:

\textbf{Condition 1 -- Continuity on $[a,b]$:}

Ensures the function has no gaps or jumps. Without this, the function might not attain all values between $f(a)$ and $f(b)$.

\textbf{Condition 2 -- Differentiability on $(a,b)$:}

Ensures the function has a well-defined tangent line everywhere inside the interval. Corners or cusps would violate this.

\textbf{Condition 3 -- Equal Endpoint Values:}

$f(a) = f(b)$ ensures the function returns to the same height, guaranteeing at least one turning point (horizontal tangent) in between.

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{If Any Condition Fails:}}

Rolle's Theorem may not apply, and there may be no horizontal tangent in $(a,b)$.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]
    % Three cases where Rolle's fails

    % Case 1: Not continuous
    \begin{scope}[shift={(0,0)}]
        \draw[->] (-0.3, 0) -- (2.5, 0) node[right] {$x$};
        \draw[->] (0, -0.3) -- (0, 2.5) node[above] {$y$};
        \draw[primaryblue, thick, domain=0:0.9, samples=30] plot (\x, {\x + 0.5});
        \draw[primaryblue, thick, domain=1.1:2, samples=30] plot (\x, {\x + 0.5});
        \filldraw[red] (0, 0.5) circle (2pt);
        \filldraw[red] (2, 2.5) circle (2pt);
        \filldraw[white, draw=primaryblue, thick] (0.9, 1.4) circle (2pt);
        \filldraw[primaryblue] (1.1, 1.6) circle (2pt);
        \node at (0, -0.4) {$a$};
        \node at (2, -0.4) {$b$};
        \node[red] at (1, -0.8) {Not continuous};
        \node[align=center] at (1, -1.3) {\small Rolle's fails};
    \end{scope}

    % Case 2: Not differentiable
    \begin{scope}[shift={(4,0)}]
        \draw[->] (-0.3, 0) -- (2.5, 0) node[right] {$x$};
        \draw[->] (0, -0.3) -- (0, 2.5) node[above] {$y$};
        \draw[primaryblue, thick, domain=0:1, samples=20] plot (\x, {abs(\x - 1) + 1});
        \draw[primaryblue, thick, domain=1:2, samples=20] plot (\x, {abs(\x - 1) + 1});
        \filldraw[red] (0, 2) circle (2pt);
        \filldraw[red] (2, 2) circle (2pt);
        \filldraw[primaryblue] (1, 1) circle (2pt);
        \node at (0, -0.4) {$a$};
        \node at (2, -0.4) {$b$};
        \node at (1, -0.4) {corner};
        \node[red] at (1, -0.8) {Not differentiable};
        \node[align=center] at (1, -1.3) {\small Rolle's fails};
    \end{scope}

    % Case 3: f(a) ≠ f(b)
    \begin{scope}[shift={(8,0)}]
        \draw[->] (-0.3, 0) -- (2.5, 0) node[right] {$x$};
        \draw[->] (0, -0.3) -- (0, 2.5) node[above] {$y$};
        \draw[primaryblue, thick, domain=0:2, samples=50] plot (\x, {0.5*\x + 0.5});
        \filldraw[red] (0, 0.5) circle (2pt);
        \filldraw[red] (2, 1.5) circle (2pt);
        \node at (0, -0.4) {$a$};
        \node at (2, -0.4) {$b$};
        \node[red] at (1, -0.8) {$f(a) \neq f(b)$};
        \node[align=center] at (1, -1.3) {\small Rolle's fails\\(no horizontal tangent)};
    \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Verify that Rolle's Theorem applies to $f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 3$ on $[1, 3]$, and find all values of $c$ guaranteed by the theorem.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Verify all three conditions

\textbf{Condition 1 (Continuous):} $f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 3$ is a polynomial, so continuous everywhere, including on $[1, 3]$. \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Condition 2 (Differentiable):} Polynomials are differentiable everywhere, including on $(1, 3)$. \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Condition 3 (Equal endpoints):}
\begin{align*}
f(1) &= 1 - 4 + 3 = 0 \\
f(3) &= 9 - 12 + 3 = 0
\end{align*}

So $f(1) = f(3) = 0$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

All three conditions satisfied! Rolle's Theorem guarantees at least one $c \in (1, 3)$ with $f'(c) = 0$.

\textbf{Step 2:} Find $c$

\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= 2x - 4 \\
f'(c) &= 0 \\
2c - 4 &= 0 \\
c &= 2
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Verify $c \in (1, 3)$

$c = 2$ and $1 < 2 < 3$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{c = 2}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Geometric Interpretation:}} The parabola $y = x^2 - 4x + 3$ has its vertex at $x = 2$. At this point, the tangent line is horizontal, which is exactly what Rolle's Theorem predicts!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Verify Rolle's Theorem for $f(x) = \sin x$ on $[0, \pi]$ and find all values of $c$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Verify conditions

\textbf{Condition 1:} $\sin x$ is continuous on $[0, \pi]$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Condition 2:} $\sin x$ is differentiable on $(0, \pi)$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Condition 3:}
\begin{align*}
f(0) &= \sin 0 = 0 \\
f(\pi) &= \sin \pi = 0
\end{align*}

So $f(0) = f(\pi)$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Step 2:} Find $c$

\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= \cos x \\
f'(c) &= 0 \\
\cos c &= 0 \\
c &= \frac{\pi}{2} \quad \text{(in the interval } (0, \pi)\text{)}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Verify $c \in (0, \pi)$

$c = \dfrac{\pi}{2}$ and $0 < \dfrac{\pi}{2} < \pi$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{c = \frac{\pi}{2}}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Geometric Check:}} The sine curve reaches its peak at $x = \frac{\pi}{2}$, where it has a horizontal tangent. This matches Rolle's prediction! \checkmark

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Explain why Rolle's Theorem does not apply to:

(a) $f(x) = |x|$ on $[-1, 1]$

(b) $f(x) = x$ on $[0, 2]$

(c) $f(x) = \dfrac{1}{x-1}$ on $[0, 2]$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} $f(x) = |x|$ on $[-1, 1]$

Check conditions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Continuous on $[-1, 1]$? Yes \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
    \item Differentiable on $(-1, 1)$? \textcolor{red}{No} -- not differentiable at $x = 0$ (corner point) \textcolor{red}{\texttimes}
    \item $f(-1) = f(1)$? Yes, both equal 1 \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Answer:} Rolle's Theorem \textbf{does not apply} because $f$ is not differentiable on $(-1, 1)$.

\textbf{Observation:} Indeed, there is no point where $f'(x) = 0$ in $(-1, 1)$ since the derivative is either $-1$ or $+1$ everywhere except at the non-differentiable point $x = 0$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} $f(x) = x$ on $[0, 2]$

Check conditions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Continuous on $[0, 2]$? Yes \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
    \item Differentiable on $(0, 2)$? Yes \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
    \item $f(0) = f(2)$? \textcolor{red}{No} -- $f(0) = 0$ but $f(2) = 2$ \textcolor{red}{\texttimes}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Answer:} Rolle's Theorem \textbf{does not apply} because $f(0) \neq f(2)$.

\textbf{Observation:} The function $f(x) = x$ has constant derivative $f'(x) = 1$, which is never zero.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(c)} $f(x) = \dfrac{1}{x-1}$ on $[0, 2]$

Check conditions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Continuous on $[0, 2]$? \textcolor{red}{No} -- discontinuous at $x = 1$ \textcolor{red}{\texttimes}
    \item (Other conditions irrelevant if continuity fails)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Answer:} Rolle's Theorem \textbf{does not apply} because $f$ is not continuous on $[0, 2]$ (vertical asymptote at $x = 1$).

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 4.2 Summary: Rolle's Theorem}}

\textbf{Rolle's Theorem:}

If $f$ is continuous on $[a,b]$, differentiable on $(a,b)$, and $f(a) = f(b)$, then:
\[
\boxed{\exists\, c \in (a, b) : f'(c) = 0}
\]

\textbf{Procedure to Apply Rolle's Theorem:}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Verify all three conditions explicitly} (continuity, differentiability, equal endpoints)
    \item Find $f'(x)$
    \item Solve $f'(c) = 0$
    \item Verify that $c \in (a, b)$
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Applications:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Proving uniqueness of roots (by contradiction)
    \item Foundation for Mean Value Theorem
    \item Theoretical tool in advanced analysis
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Not explicitly verifying all three conditions
    \item Forgetting to check $c \in (a,b)$ after finding it
    \item Confusing Rolle's Theorem with EVT or MVT
    \item Trying to apply when $f(a) \neq f(b)$
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Exam Tip:}} Always write "Check conditions:" and list all three. Examiners award marks for this even if you make an error later!

\newpage

If $f$ satisfies \textbf{both} conditions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $f$ is \textbf{continuous} on the closed interval $[a, b]$
    \item $f$ is \textbf{differentiable} on the open interval $(a, b)$
\end{enumerate}

Then there exists at least one number $c$ in $(a, b)$ such that:
\[
\boxed{f'(c) = \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a}}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Alternative Forms:}}

\textbf{Form 1 (Slope form):}
\[
f'(c) = \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a}
\]

\textbf{Form 2 (Change form):}
\[
f(b) - f(a) = f'(c)(b - a)
\]

\textbf{Form 3 (Mean value form):}
\[
f(b) = f(a) + f'(c)(b - a)
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Geometric Interpretation:}}

There exists at least one point $c$ in $(a, b)$ where the \textbf{tangent line is parallel to the secant line} connecting $(a, f(a))$ and $(b, f(b))$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Physical Interpretation:}}

If you drive from city A to city B, there must be some instant when your \textbf{instantaneous speed} equals your \textbf{average speed} for the entire trip.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Proof:}}

We reduce MVT to Rolle's Theorem using an auxiliary function.

\textbf{Step 1:} Define the auxiliary function

The secant line from $(a, f(a))$ to $(b, f(b))$ has equation:
\[
y = f(a) + \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a}(x - a)
\]

Define $g(x)$ as the \textbf{vertical distance} between $f(x)$ and the secant line:
\[
g(x) = f(x) - \left[f(a) + \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a}(x - a)\right]
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Verify Rolle's Theorem applies to $g$

\textbf{Continuity:} $g$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ since $f$ is continuous. \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Differentiability:} $g$ is differentiable on $(a,b)$ since $f$ is differentiable. \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Equal endpoints:}
\begin{align*}
g(a) &= f(a) - f(a) - 0 = 0 \\
g(b) &= f(b) - f(a) - \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}(b-a) = f(b) - f(a) - [f(b) - f(a)] = 0
\end{align*}

So $g(a) = g(b) = 0$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Step 3:} Apply Rolle's Theorem

By Rolle's Theorem, $\exists\, c \in (a,b)$ such that $g'(c) = 0$.

\textbf{Step 4:} Compute $g'(x)$ and solve

\[
g'(x) = f'(x) - \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a}
\]

Setting $g'(c) = 0$:
\begin{align*}
f'(c) - \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a} &= 0 \\
f'(c) &= \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a}
\end{align*}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} $\exists\, c \in (a,b)$ such that $f'(c) = \dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}$. \qed

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textbf{Rolle's Theorem is a Special Case of MVT:}

If $f(a) = f(b)$, then:
\[
\frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a} = \frac{0}{b - a} = 0
\]

So the MVT becomes: $f'(c) = 0$, which is exactly Rolle's Theorem!

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{In Summary:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item MVT is the \textbf{general} statement
    \item Rolle's Theorem is the \textbf{special case} when endpoints are equal
    \item Both require continuity on $[a,b]$ and differentiability on $(a,b)$
\end{itemize}

