\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} 
Find the intervals of increase and decrease for 
\[
h(x) = -x^3 + 2x^2.
\]

\textit{(From 2023 past paper, Q3 part b)}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1: Find the first derivative.}

\begin{align*}
h'(x) &= -3x^2 + 4x \\
      &= x(-3x + 4)
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2: Find the critical numbers.}

\begin{align*}
x(-3x + 4) &= 0 \\
\Rightarrow x &= 0 \quad \text{or} \quad x = \frac{4}{3}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3: Sign chart analysis.}

Test points: $x=-1$, $x=1$, $x=2$

\begin{align*}
h'(-1) &= (-1)(7) = -7 < 0 \\
h'(1)  &= (1)(1) = 1 > 0 \\
h'(2)  &= (2)(-2) = -4 < 0
\end{align*}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
& $(-\infty, 0)$ & $x = 0$ & $\left(0, \frac{4}{3}\right)$ & $x = \frac{4}{3}$ & $\left(\frac{4}{3}, \infty\right)$ \\
\hline
$h'(x)$ & $-$ & $0$ & $+$ & $0$ & $-$ \\
\hline
$h(x)$ & $\searrow$ & Min & $\nearrow$ & Max & $\searrow$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Answer:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Increasing:} $\boxed{\left(0, \frac{4}{3}\right)}$
    \item \textbf{Decreasing:} $\boxed{(-\infty, 0) \cup \left(\frac{4}{3}, \infty\right)}$
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{chemGreen}{\textbf{Bonus:}} 
Local minimum at $x=0$ and local maximum at $x=\dfrac{4}{3}$.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find where $f(x) = \dfrac{x^2}{x^2 + 1}$ is increasing and decreasing.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find $f'(x)$ using quotient rule

\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= \frac{(x^2 + 1)(2x) - x^2(2x)}{(x^2 + 1)^2} \\
&= \frac{2x^3 + 2x - 2x^3}{(x^2 + 1)^2} \\
&= \frac{2x}{(x^2 + 1)^2}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Find critical numbers

$f'(x) = 0$ when $2x = 0$, so $x = 0$.

$f'(x)$ is never undefined (denominator always positive).

\textbf{Step 3:} Sign chart

Since $(x^2 + 1)^2 > 0$ always, the sign of $f'(x)$ depends only on $2x$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
& $(-\infty, 0)$ & $x = 0$ & $(0, \infty)$ \\
\hline
$f'(x)$ & $-$ & $0$ & $+$ \\
\hline
$f(x)$ & $\searrow$ & Min & $\nearrow$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Answer:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Increasing:} \boxed{(0, \infty)}
    \item \textbf{Decreasing:} \boxed{(-\infty, 0)}
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Observation:}} $f$ has a local (and global) minimum at $x = 0$ where $f(0) = 0$.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 4.4 Summary}}

\textbf{Increasing/Decreasing Test:}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Sign of $f'(x)$} & \textbf{Behavior of $f(x)$} \\
\hline
$f'(x) > 0$ & $f$ is increasing \\
\hline
$f'(x) < 0$ & $f$ is decreasing \\
\hline
$f'(x) = 0$ & $f$ is constant \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Sign Chart Method:}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Find critical numbers: $f'(x) = 0$ or $f'$ undefined
    \item Divide number line into intervals
    \item Test sign of $f'$ in each interval
    \item Conclude increasing/decreasing based on signs
\end{enumerate}

\textcolor{red}{\textbf{Exam Alert:}} This topic appears in almost every past paper! Be comfortable creating sign charts quickly.

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Saying "$f$ is increasing at $x = c$" when you mean "on an interval containing $c$"
    \item Including critical numbers in the intervals (use open intervals!)
    \item Forgetting to check where $f'$ is undefined
    \item Not simplifying $f'$ before finding critical numbers
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Quick Check:}} If $f'$ changes from $+$ to $-$ at $c$, what does that tell you about $f(c)$?

Answer: Local maximum!

\newpage

Suppose $c$ is a critical number of a continuous function $f$.

\textbf{(a) Local Maximum:} If $f'$ changes from \textbf{positive to negative} at $c$, then $f$ has a \textbf{local maximum} at $c$.

\textbf{(b) Local Minimum:} If $f'$ changes from \textbf{negative to positive} at $c$, then $f$ has a \textbf{local minimum} at $c$.

\textbf{(c) No Extremum:} If $f'$ does \textbf{not change sign} at $c$ (stays positive or stays negative), then $f$ has \textbf{no local extremum} at $c$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Intuitive Explanation:}}

\begin{itemize}
    \item If $f'$ changes from $+$ to $-$: function goes from increasing to decreasing $\to$ peak (local max)
    \item If $f'$ changes from $-$ to $+$: function goes from decreasing to increasing $\to$ valley (local min)
    \item If $f'$ doesn't change sign: function keeps same direction $\to$ no extremum
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Important:}}

The test requires checking the sign of $f'$ on \textbf{both sides} of the critical point, not just at the critical point itself!

