\vspace{0.5cm}

To apply the First Derivative Test:

\textbf{Step 1:} Find all critical numbers of $f$ (where $f'(x) = 0$ or $f'$ undefined)

\textbf{Step 2:} Construct a sign chart for $f'(x)$:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Mark all critical numbers on a number line
    \item Test the sign of $f'$ in each interval between critical numbers
    \item Record whether $f'$ is positive or negative in each interval
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 3:} Analyze sign changes at each critical point:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Sign Change of $f'$} & \textbf{Behavior of $f$} & \textbf{Classification} \\
\hline
$+ \to -$ at $c$ & Increasing $\to$ Decreasing & Local Maximum \\
\hline
$- \to +$ at $c$ & Decreasing $\to$ Increasing & Local Minimum \\
\hline
No sign change & Same direction & No Extremum \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Step 4:} Evaluate $f(c)$ at each extremum to find the actual maximum/minimum value

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Visual Summary:}}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ccccc}
$f'$ & $+$ & $0$ & $-$ & \\
\hline
$f$ & $\nearrow$ & Max & $\searrow$ & \\
\end{tabular}
\quad
\begin{tabular}{ccccc}
$f'$ & $-$ & $0$ & $+$ & \\
\hline
$f$ & $\searrow$ & Min & $\nearrow$ & \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find and classify all local extrema of $f(x) = x^3 - 3x + 2$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find critical numbers

\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= 3x^2 - 3 = 3(x^2 - 1) = 3(x-1)(x+1)
\end{align*}

Setting $f'(x) = 0$:
\[
x = 1 \quad \text{or} \quad x = -1
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Create sign chart for $f'(x) = 3(x-1)(x+1)$

Test points: $x = -2$, $x = 0$, $x = 2$

\begin{align*}
f'(-2) &= 3(-3)(-1) = 9 > 0 \\
f'(0) &= 3(-1)(1) = -3 < 0 \\
f'(2) &= 3(1)(3) = 9 > 0
\end{align*}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
& $(-\infty, -1)$ & $x = -1$ & $(-1, 1)$ & $x = 1$ & $(1, \infty)$ \\
\hline
$f'(x)$ & $+$ & $0$ & $-$ & $0$ & $+$ \\
\hline
$f(x)$ & $\nearrow$ & \textcolor{red}{\textbf{Max}} & $\searrow$ & \textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Min}} & $\nearrow$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Step 3:} Analyze sign changes

At $x = -1$: $f'$ changes from $+$ to $-$ $\implies$ \textbf{Local Maximum}

At $x = 1$: $f'$ changes from $-$ to $+$ $\implies$ \textbf{Local Minimum}

\textbf{Step 4:} Find the extremum values

\begin{align*}
f(-1) &= (-1)^3 - 3(-1) + 2 = -1 + 3 + 2 = 4 \\
f(1) &= (1)^3 - 3(1) + 2 = 1 - 3 + 2 = 0
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Local Maximum:} \boxed{f(-1) = 4 \text{ at } x = -1}
    \item \textbf{Local Minimum:} \boxed{f(1) = 0 \text{ at } x = 1}
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find and classify all local extrema of $g(x) = x^3$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find critical numbers

\begin{align*}
g'(x) &= 3x^2
\end{align*}

Setting $g'(x) = 0$:
\[
3x^2 = 0 \implies x = 0
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Sign chart

Test points: $x = -1$, $x = 1$

\begin{align*}
g'(-1) &= 3(1) = 3 > 0 \\
g'(1) &= 3(1) = 3 > 0
\end{align*}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
& $(-\infty, 0)$ & $x = 0$ & $(0, \infty)$ \\
\hline
$g'(x)$ & $+$ & $0$ & $+$ \\
\hline
$g(x)$ & $\nearrow$ & \textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{?}} & $\nearrow$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Step 3:} Analyze sign change

At $x = 0$: $g'$ does \textbf{not change sign} (stays positive) $\implies$ \textbf{No Extremum}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\text{No local extrema}}

$x = 0$ is an inflection point, not an extremum.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Key Observation:}} Even though $g'(0) = 0$, there's no local extremum because the derivative doesn't change sign. The function $g(x) = x^3$ is strictly increasing throughout $\mathbb{R}$.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} For $f(x) = x^4 - 4x^3 + 4x^2$:

(a) Find all critical points

(b) Use the First Derivative Test to classify each

(c) Find the coordinates of all local extrema

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} Find critical points

\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= 4x^3 - 12x^2 + 8x \\
&= 4x(x^2 - 3x + 2) \\
&= 4x(x-1)(x-2)
\end{align*}

Critical points: $x = 0$, $x = 1$, $x = 2$

\textbf{(b)} First Derivative Test

Test points: $x = -1$, $x = 0.5$, $x = 1.5$, $x = 3$

\begin{align*}
f'(-1) &= 4(-1)(-2)(-3) = -24 < 0 \\
f'(0.5) &= 4(0.5)(-0.5)(-1.5) = 1.5 > 0 \\
f'(1.5) &= 4(1.5)(0.5)(-0.5) = -1.5 < 0 \\
f'(3) &= 4(3)(2)(1) = 24 > 0
\end{align*}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
& $(-\infty,0)$ & $x=0$ & $(0,1)$ & $x=1$ & $(1,2)$ & $x=2$ & $(2,\infty)$ \\
\hline
$f'$ & $-$ & $0$ & $+$ & $0$ & $-$ & $0$ & $+$ \\
\hline
$f$ & $\searrow$ & \textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Min}} & $\nearrow$ & \textcolor{red}{\textbf{Max}} & $\searrow$ & \textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Min}} & $\nearrow$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Classification:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $x = 0$: $f'$ changes $- \to +$ $\implies$ \textbf{Local Minimum}
    \item $x = 1$: $f'$ changes $+ \to -$ $\implies$ \textbf{Local Maximum}
    \item $x = 2$: $f'$ changes $- \to +$ $\implies$ \textbf{Local Minimum}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{(c)} Find coordinates

\begin{align*}
f(0) &= 0 \\
f(1) &= 1 - 4 + 4 = 1 \\
f(2) &= 16 - 32 + 16 = 0
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Local Minima:} \boxed{(0, 0) \text{ and } (2, 0)}
    \item \textbf{Local Maximum:} \boxed{(1, 1)}
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find and classify all local extrema of $h(x) = \dfrac{x^2 - 1}{x^2 + 1}$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find $h'(x)$ using quotient rule

\begin{align*}
h'(x) &= \frac{(x^2+1)(2x) - (x^2-1)(2x)}{(x^2+1)^2} \\
&= \frac{2x^3 + 2x - 2x^3 + 2x}{(x^2+1)^2} \\
&= \frac{4x}{(x^2+1)^2}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Find critical numbers

$h'(x) = 0$ when $4x = 0$, so $x = 0$.

(Denominator never zero, so $h'$ never undefined)

\textbf{Step 3:} Sign chart

Since $(x^2+1)^2 > 0$ always, sign depends only on $4x$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
& $(-\infty, 0)$ & $x = 0$ & $(0, \infty)$ \\
\hline
$h'(x)$ & $-$ & $0$ & $+$ \\
\hline
$h(x)$ & $\searrow$ & \textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Min}} & $\nearrow$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

At $x = 0$: $h'$ changes $- \to +$ $\implies$ \textbf{Local Minimum}

\textbf{Step 4:} Find minimum value

\[
h(0) = \frac{0 - 1}{0 + 1} = -1
\]

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\text{Local minimum at } (0, -1)}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Note:}} This is also a global minimum since $h(x) \to 1$ as $x \to \pm\infty$, and $h(x) \geq -1$ for all $x$.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 4.5 Summary: First Derivative Test}}

\textbf{Classification Rules:}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Sign Change of $f'$ at $c$} & \textbf{$f(c)$ is a} & \textbf{Symbol} \\
\hline
$+$ to $-$ & Local Maximum & $\cap$ \\
\hline
$-$ to $+$ & Local Minimum & $\cup$ \\
\hline
No change & No Extremum & $\nearrow$ or $\searrow$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Procedure:}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Find all critical numbers ($f'(x) = 0$ or $f'$ undefined)
    \item Create sign chart for $f'$
    \item Check sign change at each critical point
    \item Evaluate $f$ at extrema to find values
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Advantages of First Derivative Test:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Works even when $f''$ doesn't exist
    \item Simultaneously determines intervals of increase/decrease
    \item More intuitive (based on function behavior)
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Testing $f'$ \textit{at} the critical point instead of on both \textit{sides}
    \item Forgetting to check critical points where $f'$ is undefined
    \item Concluding there's an extremum just because $f'(c) = 0$
    \item Not checking \textit{both} sides of the critical point
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Memory Aid:}} ``Up to down = max on top, down to up = min at bottom''

\newpage

Let $f$ be differentiable on an interval $I$.

\textbf{Concave Up:} $f$ is \textbf{concave up} on $I$ if $f'$ is \textbf{increasing} on $I$.

\textbf{Concave Down:} $f$ is \textbf{concave down} on $I$ if $f'$ is \textbf{decreasing} on $I$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Visual Interpretation:}}

\textbf{Concave Up:} The graph curves upward, like $\cup$
\begin{itemize}
    \item Curve ``holds water''
    \item Tangent lines lie \textbf{below} the curve
    \item Slope increases from left to right
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Concave Down:} The graph curves downward, like $\cap$
\begin{itemize}
    \item Curve ``spills water''
    \item Tangent lines lie \textbf{above} the curve
    \item Slope decreases from left to right
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\begin{center}
