\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} For $f(x) = 2x^3 - 3x^2 - 12x$:

(a) Find all inflection points

(b) Determine where $f$ is concave up and concave down

(From 2024 past paper, Q2 part a)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} Find inflection points

\textbf{Step 1:} Find $f''(x)$

\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= 6x^2 - 6x - 12 \\
f''(x) &= 12x - 6
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Solve $f''(x) = 0$

\begin{align*}
12x - 6 &= 0 \\
x &= \frac{1}{2}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Verify concavity changes

Test points: $x = 0$ (left) and $x = 1$ (right)

\begin{align*}
f''(0) &= 12(0) - 6 = -6 < 0 \quad \text{(concave down)} \\
f''(1) &= 12(1) - 6 = 6 > 0 \quad \text{(concave up)}
\end{align*}

Concavity changes from down to up at $x = \frac{1}{2}$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Step 4:} Find $y$-coordinate

\begin{align*}
f\left(\frac{1}{2}\right) &= 2\left(\frac{1}{8}\right) - 3\left(\frac{1}{4}\right) - 12\left(\frac{1}{2}\right) \\
&= \frac{1}{4} - \frac{3}{4} - 6 \\
&= -\frac{1}{2} - 6 \\
&= -\frac{13}{2}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer (a):} \boxed{\text{Inflection point at } \left(\frac{1}{2}, -\frac{13}{2}\right)}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} Concavity intervals

From the sign chart:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Interval} & \textbf{$f''(x)$} & \textbf{Concavity} \\
\hline
$\left(-\infty, \frac{1}{2}\right)$ & $-$ & Concave Down $\cap$ \\
\hline
$\left(\frac{1}{2}, \infty\right)$ & $+$ & Concave Up $\cup$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Answer (b):}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Concave Up:} \boxed{\left(\frac{1}{2}, \infty\right)}
    \item \textbf{Concave Down:} \boxed{\left(-\infty, \frac{1}{2}\right)}
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} For $h(x) = -x^3 + 2x^2$, find:

(a) Intervals of concavity

(b) All inflection points

(From 2023 past paper, Q3 part b)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find $h''(x)$

\begin{align*}
h'(x) &= -3x^2 + 4x \\
h''(x) &= -6x + 4
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Solve $h''(x) = 0$

\begin{align*}
-6x + 4 &= 0 \\
x &= \frac{2}{3}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Sign chart for $h''(x) = -6x + 4$

Test points: $x = 0$ and $x = 1$

\begin{align*}
h''(0) &= 4 > 0 \\
h''(1) &= -6 + 4 = -2 < 0
\end{align*}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Interval} & \textbf{$h''(x)$} & \textbf{Concavity} \\
\hline
$\left(-\infty, \frac{2}{3}\right)$ & $+$ & Concave Up $\cup$ \\
\hline
$\left(\frac{2}{3}, \infty\right)$ & $-$ & Concave Down $\cap$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Answer (a):}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Concave Up:} \boxed{\left(-\infty, \frac{2}{3}\right)}
    \item \textbf{Concave Down:} \boxed{\left(\frac{2}{3}, \infty\right)}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 4:} Find inflection point

Concavity changes at $x = \frac{2}{3}$, so it's an inflection point.

$y$-coordinate:
\begin{align*}
h\left(\frac{2}{3}\right) &= -\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^3 + 2\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^2 \\
&= -\frac{8}{27} + 2 \cdot \frac{4}{9} \\
&= -\frac{8}{27} + \frac{8}{9} \\
&= -\frac{8}{27} + \frac{24}{27} \\
&= \frac{16}{27}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer (b):} \boxed{\text{Inflection point at } \left(\frac{2}{3}, \frac{16}{27}\right)}

\vspace{0.5cm}

but No Inflection Point}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Show that $f(x) = x^4$ has $f''(0) = 0$, but $x = 0$ is not an inflection point.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find $f''(x)$

\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= 4x^3 \\
f''(x) &= 12x^2
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Verify $f''(0) = 0$

\[
f''(0) = 12(0)^2 = 0 \quad \checkmark
\]

\textbf{Step 3:} Check if concavity changes at $x = 0$

Test points: $x = -1$ and $x = 1$

\begin{align*}
f''(-1) &= 12(1) = 12 > 0 \quad \text{(concave up)} \\
f''(1) &= 12(1) = 12 > 0 \quad \text{(concave up)}
\end{align*}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
& $(-\infty, 0)$ & $x = 0$ & $(0, \infty)$ \\
\hline
$f''(x)$ & $+$ & $0$ & $+$ \\
\hline
Concavity & Up & ? & Up \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Conclusion:} Concavity does \textbf{not change} at $x = 0$ (stays concave up throughout).

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{x = 0 \text{ is NOT an inflection point}}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Key Lesson:}} $f''(c) = 0$ is a \textbf{necessary} condition for inflection point, but not \textbf{sufficient}. Must verify sign change!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 4.6 Summary: Concavity and Inflection Points}}

\textbf{Concavity Test:}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Sign of $f''(x)$} & \textbf{Concavity} & \textbf{Shape} \\
\hline
$f'' > 0$ & Concave Up & $\cup$ \\
\hline
$f'' < 0$ & Concave Down & $\cap$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Finding Inflection Points:}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Find where $f''(x) = 0$ or $f''$ is undefined
    \item Check if $f''$ changes sign at those points
    \item If yes: inflection point. If no: not an inflection point
    \item Find $y$-coordinate: $y = f(c)$
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Visual Memory Aid:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Concave up: ``smile'' $\cup$ (holds water)
    \item Concave down: ``frown'' $\cap$ (spills water)
    \item Inflection point: where curve changes from smile to frown (or vice versa)
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{red}{\textbf{Exam Alert:}} This topic appears in almost every past paper! Always verify sign change.

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Assuming $f''(c) = 0$ automatically means inflection point
    \item Not verifying that concavity actually changes
    \item Confusing inflection points with extrema
    \item Forgetting to check where $f''$ is undefined
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Quick Test:}} If you can draw a tangent line that crosses the curve at a point, that point is likely an inflection point!

\newpage

Suppose $f''$ is continuous near $c$ and $f'(c) = 0$.

\textbf{(a) Local Maximum:} If $f''(c) < 0$, then $f$ has a \textbf{local maximum} at $c$.

\textbf{(b) Local Minimum:} If $f''(c) > 0$, then $f$ has a \textbf{local minimum} at $c$.

\textbf{(c) Inconclusive:} If $f''(c) = 0$, the test is \textbf{inconclusive}. Use the First Derivative Test instead.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Intuitive Explanation:}}

\begin{itemize}
    \item If $f''(c) < 0$: curve is concave down at $c$ $\to$ looks like $\cap$ $\to$ peak $\to$ local max
    \item If $f''(c) > 0$: curve is concave up at $c$ $\to$ looks like $\cup$ $\to$ valley $\to$ local min
    \item If $f''(c) = 0$: concavity unclear at $c$ $\to$ test fails $\to$ need more analysis
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Relationship to Concavity:}}

The Second Derivative Test essentially checks the concavity at the critical point:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Concave down ($f'' < 0$) at a critical point $\implies$ local max
    \item Concave up ($f'' > 0$) at a critical point $\implies$ local min
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Critical Requirements:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Must have $f'(c) = 0$ first! (The test only applies at critical points)
    \item If $f''(c) = 0$, the test gives \textbf{no information}
    \item If $f''(c)$ doesn't exist, use First Derivative Test
\end{itemize}

