\vspace{0.5cm}

\textbf{Vertical Asymptotes:}

The line $x = a$ is a vertical asymptote if:
\[
\lim_{x \to a^+} f(x) = \pm\infty \quad \text{or} \quad \lim_{x \to a^-} f(x) = \pm\infty
\]

\textbf{Where to look:} Points where denominator = 0 (for rational functions)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Horizontal Asymptotes:}

The line $y = L$ is a horizontal asymptote if:
\[
\lim_{x \to +\infty} f(x) = L \quad \text{or} \quad \lim_{x \to -\infty} f(x) = L
\]

\textbf{For rational functions} $\dfrac{p(x)}{q(x)}$:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\deg(p) < \deg(q)$: H.A. is $y = 0$
    \item $\deg(p) = \deg(q)$: H.A. is $y = \dfrac{\text{leading coeff of } p}{\text{leading coeff of } q}$
    \item $\deg(p) > \deg(q)$: No H.A. (possibly oblique asymptote)
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Oblique (Slant) Asymptotes:}

If $\deg(p) = \deg(q) + 1$, use polynomial long division:
\[
\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} = mx + b + \frac{r(x)}{q(x)}
\]

The line $y = mx + b$ is the oblique asymptote.

