\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the point on the parabola $y = x^2$ that is closest to the point $(3, 0)$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1--2: Setup}

Point on parabola: $(x, x^2)$

Point given: $(3, 0)$

Distance: $D = \sqrt{(x-3)^2 + (x^2-0)^2}$

\textbf{Step 3--4: Objective}

Minimize $D$, or equivalently minimize $D^2$ (easier):

\[
f(x) = D^2 = (x-3)^2 + x^4
\]

\textbf{Step 5--6: Domain}

$x \in (-\infty, \infty)$

\textbf{Step 7: Critical points}

\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= 2(x-3) + 4x^3 \\
&= 4x^3 + 2x - 6
\end{align*}

Test $x = 1$: $f'(1) = 4 + 2 - 6 = 0$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

Factor: $f'(x) = 2(2x^3 + x - 3) = 2(x-1)(2x^2 + 2x + 3)$

The quadratic $2x^2 + 2x + 3$ has no real roots (discriminant < 0).

Only critical point: $x = 1$

\textbf{Step 8: Verify minimum}

$f''(x) = 12x^2 + 2$

$f''(1) = 14 > 0 \implies$ minimum

\textbf{Step 9: Answer}

When $x = 1$: $y = 1^2 = 1$

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\text{Closest point is } (1, 1)}

Distance: $D = \sqrt{(1-3)^2 + 1^2} = \sqrt{5} \approx 2.24$ units

